// Bot message handler -- fires on every reply the user types to the bot.
// Deluge bots declare no permissions: `user`, `message` and `chat` arrive
// automatically. Keeping the code minimal keeps the consent dialog small.
//
// Shapes, confirmed by dumping them in a live handler:
//   message -> a plain STRING, the text itself (NOT a map; .get() throws)
//   user    -> Map with id, email, first_name, organization_id, ...
//   chat    -> Map with id, chat_type, and recent_messages[]
//
// Deliberately minimal: enqueue and return. Any platform component is capped
// at 15 seconds, and the real work (deciding what the reply means, resuming a
// session) belongs on the laptop, not here.

payload = Map();
payload.put("cliq_user", user.get("id"));
payload.put("text", message.toString());

// The Cliq message id is what makes a repeated delivery idempotent on the
// relay. It is not passed directly, but the triggering message is the most
// recent entry in chat.recent_messages. If that shape ever changes, the
// relay generates its own id rather than failing.
recent = chat.get("recent_messages");
if(recent != null && recent.size() > 0)
{
	payload.put("message_id", recent.get(recent.size() - 1).get("id"));
}

headers = Map();
headers.put("Content-Type", "application/json");
headers.put("X-Relay-Secret", "REPLACE_WITH_RELAY_INBOUND_SECRET");

// Wrapped: an unhandled invokeurl failure aborts the whole script silently,
// which looks identical to the handler never running.
try
{
	invokeurl
	[
		url: "REPLACE_WITH_RELAY_BASE/cliq-inbound"
		type: POST
		parameters: payload.toString()
		headers: headers
	];
}
catch (e)
{
	response = Map();
	response.put("text", "Couldn't reach Claude Code just now: " + e.toString());
	return response;
}

// Returning an empty map posts nothing back. The laptop answers in its own
// time -- an immediate "got it" here would just be noise on every reply.
return Map();
